    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d390">

                <pb facs="0507" n="439" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v01/pg439"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72/1780½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the Counselor for the Department of
                        State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Lansing</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-06-07">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 7, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Lansing</hi>: Thank you for letting me see
                        this.<anchor n="98" xml:id="d390-f1"/> I have no doubt that it interprets a
                    very large element of opinion in the country, but I do not think that anything
                    that we are doing would exclude temperate action.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Woodrow Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d390-f1" n="98" xml:id="d390-f1-content">
                <p>Mr. Lansing had forwarded to President Wilson a telegram from Mr. Daniel F.
                    Kellogg, of New York, in which arbitration of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Lusitania</hi> case was suggested and in which it was stated that “people
                    here would be strongly adverse to any government action that would make war
                    possible on any grounds that have been disclosed to date.” (File No.
                    763.72/1779½.)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
